TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Orbit; Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R7","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Orbit (Images 1–5); Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"At 600 km altitude, a sun-synchronous orbit requires a retrograde inclination of approximately 97.8°, not 87.79°. The stated inclination is prograde and would not produce the claimed sun-synchronous nodal precession; the plotted curve itself is near 97.8° at 600 km."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The semi-major axis is inconsistent with the stated Earth radius and altitude: 6371 km + 600 km = 6971 km, not 7171 km."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Kourou is near 5.2° N, not 45.05° N. This also invalidates the stated comparison of the launch-site latitude with the target inclination."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"RAAN is not determined by the launch facility's location as stated; it is an inertial orbital orientation that depends on launch timing and epoch."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The reported mean access duration of 580.786 seconds is about 9.7 minutes, not almost twenty minutes."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The report assumes Mount Pleasant Radio Observatory can provide spacecraft communications without establishing suitable TT&C antennas, frequencies, or link performance. A sun-synchronous orbit also does not guarantee continuous sunlight or a daylight descending track; local solar time and eclipse conditions are not specified."}],"overall":"A clearly structured concept with useful STK visualisations and coverage of the requested mission segments. The central orbit has substantial calculation and interpretation errors, while operator/customer roles, ground-station capability, and supporting software files are incomplete. The design therefore needs significant technical correction before it can support its stated mission objectives.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The Great Barrier Reef monitoring mission and its environmental rationale are explained in detail, and the single-spacecraft choice is justified. Marine and conservation scientists are identified as users, but the responsible operator and specific customer organisations are not clearly named.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"A near-real-time relay to monitoring stations on the ground would allow marine and conservation scientists to more accurately predict and react to coral bleaching events."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required. This also just allows for simplicity in communications, reduces cost, and requires less time to build and launch."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":24.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report covers launch, orbit, ground access, mission duration and disposal, and includes STK imagery and access results. However, major orbital-element errors undermine the central design; the ground-station communications claim is not adequately substantiated, and no STK project files are provided. The work shows some design understanding but is not a reliable, comprehensive mission design.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 87.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost twenty minutes was achieved, in addition to the already frequent amount of passes."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion summarises the proposed launch, orbit and ground facility, and acknowledges a launch-vehicle risk. It is mostly a recap, however, and its positive conclusions are not well supported given the orbit calculation errors and limited evidence for imaging and communications performance.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"Thus, launch may need to be adjusted if the rocket is still not constructed by the proposed date."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":10.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a logical structure, contents list, executive summary, headings, figures, and bibliography. Presentation is not fully professional: prose and formatting are inconsistent, figures and tables lack clear numbering, references are incomplete, and Wikipedia is used despite the task excluding it as an acceptable source.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."}]}]}
